window.EditableMathsPilot=Object.assign(window.EditableMathsPilot||{},{"WV-MATHS-APPLICATIONS-2016-ASSUMED-Q15":{"question":"<p>An express bus service links shopping centres A–G according to the adjacency matrix.</p><table><tr><th></th><th>A</th><th>B</th><th>C</th><th>D</th><th>E</th><th>F</th><th>G</th></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>E</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>F</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>G</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td></tr></table>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Draw the undirected network.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"drawing","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)(i)","text":"<p>What matrix feature shows services run in both directions?</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)(ii)","text":"<p>How many one-stage journeys leave B?</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)(iii)","text":"<p>List all two-stage one-way journeys leaving B.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Edges AB, AE, AG, BC, BD, BE, CD, CF, FG; award for at least 3, at least 6, then all 9.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(i)</td><td>The matrix is symmetric about its main diagonal: aᵢⱼ=aⱼᵢ.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(ii)</td><td>4.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(iii)</td><td>B–A–E, B–A–G, B–C–D, B–C–F, B–D–C, B–E–A; excludes return trips to B.</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":8,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-APPLICATIONS-2021-ASSUMED-Q10":{"question":"<p>Wendy borrows$16000 and pays$600 at each month’s end. The loan spreadsheet is supplied.</p><table><tr><th>Month</th><th>Opening($)</th><th>Interest($)</th><th>Payment($)</th><th>Closing($)</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>16000.00</td><td>98.67</td><td>600.00</td><td>15498.67</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>15498.67</td><td>95.58</td><td>600.00</td><td>14994.24</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>14994.24</td><td>92.46</td><td>600.00</td><td>14486.71</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></table>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Show the nominal annual interest rate is about 7.4%.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Complete row 4.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Write a recurrence for month-end balance.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>How many months until repayment?</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(e)","text":"<p>Find total interest paid.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(f)","text":"<p>If she could pay$800 monthly over the same repayment time and rate, find the maximum initial loan.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Monthly rate 98.67/16000; multiply by 12×100 to get≈7.4%.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Month 4: opening$14486.71, interest$89.33, payment$600, closing$13976.04. Award for opening/payment, interest and closing.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>T₀=16000; Tₙ₊₁=(1+0.074/12)Tₙ−600.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>30 months.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(e)</td><td>29 payments of$600 plus final$134.44=$17534.44; interest=$1534.44. Award for final partial payment, total and subtraction.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(f)</td><td>PV=800[1−(1+0.074/12)<sup>−30</sup>]/(0.074/12)≈$21849.52. Award for periods/rate, annuity formula and result.</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":14,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2023-FREE-Q05":{"question":"<p>The table gives polynomial f, its derivatives, and F(x)=∫<sub>0</sub><sup>x</sup>f(t)dt. There are no stationary points at non-integer x.</p><table><tr><th>x</th><th>0</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th><th>4</th><th>5</th><th>6</th></tr><tr><td>f(x)</td><td>a</td><td>b</td><td>4</td><td>c</td><td>0</td><td>d</td><td>e</td></tr><tr><td>f′(x)</td><td>16</td><td>0</td><td>-4</td><td>-2</td><td>0</td><td>-4</td><td>-20</td></tr><tr><td>f″(x)</td><td>-24</td><td>-9</td><td>0</td><td>3</td><td>0</td><td>-9</td><td>-24</td></tr><tr><td>F(x)</td><td>0</td><td>4.7</td><td>10.4</td><td>12.6</td><td>12.8</td><td>12.5</td><td>7.2</td></tr></table>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Find d(f(x)²)/dx at x=2.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Evaluate ∫<sub>2</sub><sup>4</sup>[f(x)+2]dx.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Find d/dx[∫<sub>2</sub><sup>x</sup>f(t)dt] at x=2.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>Find stationary x-values and classify them with reasons.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(e)","text":"<p>Sketch a possible f on 0≤x≤6.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"drawing","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>2 f(2)f′(2)=2×4×(−4)=−32.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>F(4)−F(2)+2(4−2)=12.8−10.4+4=6.4. Award for linearity, FTC and result.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>FTC gives f(2)=4.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>x=1 is a local maximum because f″(1)=−9. x=4 is a stationary inflection: f′ remains negative on both sides. Award for stationary values and both justified classifications.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(e)</td><td>Passes(2,4),(4,0); maximum at 1 and stationary inflection at 4; correct concavity, with another inflection at 2. One mark per feature group.</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":13,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2022-ASSUMED-Q19":{"question":"<p>A ramp passes through A(6,0,0), B(0,2,0), C(0,0,3). A lamp is at L=(2,2,2.5); dimensions metres.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Find the Cartesian ramp equation.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Find the most brightly illuminated point, nearest to L.</p>","marks":4,"lines":12,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>A ball rolls 1 metre from C along steepest descent. Find its displacement vector s to 0.001.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>x/6+y/2+z/3=1, or x+3 y+2 z=6.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Normal n=(1,3,2); line from L is L+λn. Substitute in plane:13+14λ=6, so λ=−½; nearest point(1.5,0.5,1.5). Award for normal line, substitution, parameter and point.</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Descent direction in plane is proportional to(1,3,−5), perpendicular to horizontal contours. Unit vector s=(1,3,−5)/√35≈(0.169,0.507,−0.845). Award for direction, normalisation and components.</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\"><img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":9,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-APPLICATIONS-2016-ASSUMED-Q09":{"question":"<p>The directed pipe network has capacities in litres/minute, with source A and sink F.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>List flow paths and determine maximum delivery to F.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Show a minimum cut verifying this maximum.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Label a feasible maximum flow on each pipe.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>How much water passes through C at that maximum?</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(e)","text":"<p>If C can handle only 480 L/min, which single pipe should be enlarged by the least amount to preserve maximum flow?</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>ABEF100, ADF100, ACF350, ACEF200; total 750 L/min. Award for some paths, all feasible contributions and maximum.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>A minimum cut crosses AB, CE, CF, DF:100+200+350+100=750.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>AB100, BE100, AC550, CE200, CF350, AD100, DF100, DC0, EF300.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>550 L/min.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(e)</td><td>Increase DF by 70 L/min, from 100 to 170, diverting the 70 lost through C.</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\"><img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":8,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-APPLICATIONS-2020-ASSUMED-Q09":{"question":"<p>Giuseppe’s quarterly annuity satisfies Aₙ₊₁=1.019 Aₙ−P, A₀=Q. The spreadsheet starts with$648000, first investment gain$12312, and quarterly payment$15000.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Find P, Q, X(first closing balance), Y(second-quarter investment gain).</p>","marks":4,"lines":12,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Find the nominal annual interest rate.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>When the balance first falls below$300000, he converts to a perpetuity. How many years does the annuity run?</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>Find the new quarterly perpetuity payment.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(e)","text":"<p>Is the effective annual return 7.93%? Calculate and comment.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>P=$15000, Q=$648000, X=$645312, Y=$12260.93; one mark each.</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>4×1.9%=7.6% p. a.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>First below$300000 at quarter 66, balance≈$299501.07;16.5 years.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>0.019×299501.07≈$5690.52 quarterly.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(e)</td><td>(1.019)<sup>4</sup>−1≈7.82%, so 7.93% is incorrect.</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":11,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2020-ASSUMED-Q19":{"question":"<p>Sardine population P(t)=2.4/[1+239 e<sup>−0.3 t</sup>] million tonnes is graphed.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Find P(0).</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Express P′=rP(k−P), stating r, k.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>At 500000 tonnes estimate next month’s population change using increments.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>Find maximum growth rate.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(e)","text":"<p>If initial population were 1.3 million tonnes with the same growth law, sketch and explain the new population graph.</p>","marks":2,"lines":8,"space":"drawing","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>2.4/240=0.01 million tonnes=10000 tonnes.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>r=0.3/2.4=0.125, k=2.4.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Δt=1/12 year; ΔP≈0.125(0.5)(2.4−0.5)/12≈0.009896 million tonnes≈9896 tonnes. Award for units, increments and result.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Maximum atP=k/2=1.2; rate=rk²/4=0.18 million tonnes/year.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(e)</td><td>Starts at 1.3, increases toward 2.4 and is concave down throughout because it starts above the inflection population 1.2.</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\"><img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":11,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2023-ASSUMED-Q14":{"question":"<p>A20 m-long dam has a uniform cross-section, flat base 0≤x≤5 and right side y=(x−5)²/4. Its maximum water depth is 10 m.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Use calculus to show V(h)=100 h+(80/3)h<sup>3/2</sup>.</p>","marks":5,"lines":15,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Use increments for the volume change as depth rises 6 to 6.1 m.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Initial volume 1000 m³; winter inflow V<sub>R</sub>~N(600,200²)m³. With no losses, find the probability of reaching full capacity.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>At height y, width=5+2√y. Thus V=20∫<sub>0</sub><sup>h</sup>(5+2√y)dy=100 h+(80/3)h<sup>3/2</sup>. Award for inversion, width, definite integral, integration and simplification.</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>V′(h)=100+40√h; ΔV≈V′(6)(0.1)=10+4√6≈19.798 m³. Award for derivative, increment and result.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Full capacity V(10)≈1843.274 m³; requires V<sub>R</sub>≥843.274. Normal upper tail≈0.1119. Award for capacity, event and probability.</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":11,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2018-ASSUMED-Q18":{"question":"<p>A university has 1600 people. At 8 am,100 know a rumour; N′=kN(1600−N), and the initial spread rate is 60 people/hour. t is hours after 8 am.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Show k=0.0004.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>At 11 am about 500 know it. Use increments to estimate new people hearing it by 11.15 am.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Using partial fractions, separate variables to show N/(1600−N)=e<sup>0.64 t</sup>/15.</p>","marks":4,"lines":12,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>Find the time of fastest spread, nearest minute.</p>","marks":4,"lines":12,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>60=k×100×1500 gives k=0.0004.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>AtN=500, rate=0.0004×500×1100=220/hour; Δt=¼hour; ΔN≈55 people. Award for rate, units and estimate.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Integrates[1/N+1/(1600−N)]dN/1600=0.0004 dt. Thus ln[N/(1600−N)]=0.64 t+C; N(0)=100 gives e<sup>C</sup>=1/15. Award for separation, integration, initial constant and exponentiation.</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Logistic rate is maximal atN=800. Then 1=e<sup>0.64 t</sup>/15, so t=ln 15/0.64≈4.2313 hours;12.14 pm. Award for midpoint, equation, time and clock conversion.</td><td>4</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":13,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-APPLICATIONS-2022-ASSUMED-Q12":{"question":"<p>The directed commuter network has stairs, escalators and walkways, with capacities in people/minute.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)(i)","text":"<p>Identify the bus-stop vertex.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(a)(ii)","text":"<p>Identify the train-platform vertex.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>List flow routes and determine maximum throughput.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Draw a corresponding minimum cut.</p>","marks":1,"lines":8,"space":"drawing","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>One 120-person/minute escalator may be increased to 150. Which should be upgraded? Justify.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)(i)</td><td>F.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)(ii)</td><td>C.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>One allocation: FGHABC35, FGHJ KBC50, FJKBC70, FEDJC80, FJC50, FEDC40. Total 325 people/minute. Award for systematic allocation, feasible paths and total.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>A minimum cut crosses AB, HJ, FJ, ED, capacities 35+50+120+120=325.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Upgrade FJ: it has sufficient spare inflow and outflow to use the extra 30. GH or KB remain limited elsewhere; ED can add only 25. Award for choice and comparison.</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\"><img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":8,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2019-ASSUMED-Q14":{"question":"<p>Truck service times have population mean 80 seconds and SD20 seconds. A random sample has 100 trucks.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>State and justify the approximate sample-mean distribution.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Find P(T̄&gt;83).</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Would more trucks increase or decrease(b)? Explain.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>Find minimum n with P(80≤T̄≤82)&gt;0.40.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>By CLT, T̄≈N(80,20²/100)=N(80,4). Award for normality, parameters and CLT.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>P(Z&gt;1.5)≈0.0668.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Decreases because standard error falls, while 83 stays above the mean.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>P(0≤Z≤√n/10)&gt;0.4 requires√n/10&gt;Φ<sup>−1</sup>(0.9)=1.28155. Thus n&gt;164.24; minimum 165. Award for quantile, inequality and rounding.</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":10,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2017-ASSUMED-Q14":{"question":"<p>Drone position is r(t)=(100+0.5 t,0.6 t,50−0.02 t)metres, t seconds. Tower top is(200,150,30). Interference occurs within 50 m of the tower top.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Find drone height after 2 minutes.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Give its position relative to the tower top.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Does it cause interference? If so, for how long, nearest second?</p>","marks":4,"lines":12,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Uses t=120; height 47.6 m.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>(−100+0.5 t,−150+0.6 t,20−0.02 t).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Sets squared relative distance&lt;2500:0.6104 t²−280.8 t+30400&lt;0. Roots≈174.312 and 285.714 seconds. Interference lasts≈111 seconds. Award for distance expression, inequality, roots and duration.</td><td>4</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":7,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-APPLICATIONS-2016-ASSUMED-Q13":{"question":"<p>Simon invests$5000 without withdrawals.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)(i)","text":"<p>Find its value after 3 years at 6.5% p. a., compounded annually.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(a)(ii)","text":"<p>Find doubling time at 6.5% p. a., compounded monthly.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>OptionA earns 5.5% p. a. monthly, effective 5.64%. OptionB earns 5.4% p. a. daily. Find B’s effective annual rate to 2 decimals and choose the better option.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)(i)</td><td>5000(1.065)³≈$6039.75.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)(ii)</td><td>Solves(1+0.065/12)<sup>12 t</sup>=2; t≈10.69 years (129 complete monthly periods).</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>(1+0.054/365)<sup>365</sup>−1≈5.55%; OptionA’s 5.64% is greater. Award for equivalent-rate formula, value and comparison.</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":6,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-APPLICATIONS-2021-ASSUMED-Q16":{"question":"<p>The water network has source S, sink T and pipe capacities in kL/hour.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)(i)","text":"<p>Find the indicated cut value.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(a)(ii)","text":"<p>What does it imply about maximum flow?</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Find maximum flows on paths SABT and SCDT.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>List feasible flow paths giving the maximum total.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>Label a corresponding flow on all pipes.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(e)","text":"<p>Draw a minimum cut.</p>","marks":1,"lines":8,"space":"drawing","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)(i)</td><td>45 kL/hour.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)(ii)</td><td>Maximum flow cannot exceed 45 kL/hour.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>SABT10; SCDT14 kL/hour.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>SABT10, SCDT14, SACEDT3, SACET4, SET9; total 40 kL/hour. Award for additional paths, feasible allocation and total.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Labels the edge sums from that allocation, with total outflow from S and inflow to T both 40. Award for conservation and all labels.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(e)</td><td>A minimum cut separates {S, A, C} from the other vertices; forward capacities AB10+CD14+CE7+SE9=40, with reverse BC contributing 0.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\"><img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\"><img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":10,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2022-ASSUMED-Q12":{"question":"<p>A random sample R₁of 100 engine cylinders has sample SD1 mm. Population mean diameter is μ; X̄is the sample mean.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>State X̄’s approximate distribution and parameters.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Find P(|X̄−μ|&gt;0.2), to 0.001.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Find the width of a 95% confidence interval, to 0.001 mm.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>A manager proposes duplicating the same observations to claim n=200 and narrow the interval. State two major problems.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Approximately normal by CLT, meanμ and estimated variance 1²/100=0.01 mm². Award for model, mean and error.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>2 P(Z&gt;2)≈0.046.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Width=2(1.96)(1/√100)=0.392 mm.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Duplicates are not independent new measurements.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>No additional information is collected, so treating 200 rows as sample size 200 understates the true uncertainty.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":9,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2018-ASSUMED-Q19":{"question":"<p>A rocket starts at ground level with launch speed 70 m/second at angleθto horizontal, with steady horizontal wind speed w. Gravity gives a(t)=(0,−9.8).</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Show r(t)=((70 cosθ+w)t,70 t sinθ−4.9 t²).</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Find the Cartesian trajectory in θ, w.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>For w=2, find the launch angle maximising range, to 0.1°.</p>","marks":4,"lines":12,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Integrates acceleration, uses initial velocity(70 cosθ+w,70 sinθ)and zero initial position to obtain the stated r. One mark per stage.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Eliminates t=x/(70 cosθ+w): y=70 x sinθ/(70 cosθ+w)−4.9 x²/(70 cosθ+w)².</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Flight time=140 sinθ/9.8; range=(70 cosθ+2)140 sinθ/9.8. Differentiate and solve 70 cos 2θ+2 cosθ=0, choosing the maximum in 0&lt;θ&lt;90°. Optimum≈45.6°. Award for flight time/range, derivative, valid root and maximum.</td><td>4</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":9,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2019-ASSUMED-Q15":{"question":"<p>A wave window has upper edge h₁(x)=4−4(x−1)²,0≤x≤1, joined to h₂(x)=a[cos(x−1)+1],1&lt; x≤d, ending at ground level.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Determine a.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Find the bottom-edge length.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Find glass volume for uniform thickness 3 cm.</p>","marks":5,"lines":15,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>A tangent line AB touches the right-hand curve at C; A is 1.39 m higher than B. Find C’s height.</p>","marks":4,"lines":12,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Continuity at 1 requires h₁(1)=4=h₂(1)=2 a; a=2. Award for endpoint height, continuity and solution.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>First zero of 2[cos(x−1)+1]is d=1+π, so length≈4.142 m.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Area=∫<sub>0</sub><sup>1</sup>[4−4(x−1)²]dx+∫<sub>1</sub><sup>1+π</sup>2[cos(x−1)+1]dx=8/3+2π. Volume=0.03(8/3+2π)≈0.26850 m³. Award for two integrals, evaluations, thickness conversion and volume.</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Slope AB=−1.39/(1+π). Sets h₂′(x)=−2 sin(x−1)equal to this slope and chooses the upper touch point; h(C)≈3.97 m. Award for line slope, derivative, valid location and height.</td><td>4</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\"><img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":14,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2017-ASSUMED-Q17":{"question":"<p>A spring mass satisfies x″=−π²x, initially x(0)=0 and x′(0)=8πcm/second.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Determine x(t).</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Find distance travelled in the first 5 seconds.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Friction changes amplitude according to A′=−0.4 A, A(0)=8 cm. Determine A(t).</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>It appears stopped when amplitude drops below 0.01 cm. Find the time, to 0.1 second.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>SHMfrequencyπ; sine phase from x(0)=0; amplitude 8 from initial velocity. x=8 sinπt. One mark per stage.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Period 2 seconds;5 seconds=2.5 cycles. Distance=2.5×4×8=80 cm. Award for period, number of cycles and distance.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>A=8 e<sup>−0.4 t</sup>.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Solves 8 e<sup>−0.4 t</sup>=0.01; t=ln 800/0.4≈16.7 seconds. Award for equation, logarithms and time.</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":11,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2021-ASSUMED-Q10":{"question":"<p>Lucky 7 tickets have two random panels as shown; scratch one number per panel. A total of 7 or more wins. At event 1,124 of 400 tickets win.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Find the observed winning proportion.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Show the true winning probability is 7/24.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Find the mean and SD of the sample winning proportion for 400 tickets.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>At event 2,400 tickets are bought. If its sample proportion is 0.6 standard deviations from the true proportion, how many prizes could be won?</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>124/400=0.31.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>From panel frequencies, P(total 7)=8/48, P(total 8)=4/48, P(total 9)=2/48. Sum 14/48=7/24. Award for weighted outcome probabilities and sum.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Mean=7/24; SD=√[(7/24)(17/24)/400]≈0.02273.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Distance from true proportion=0.6×0.02273≈0.01364; proportions≈0.29167±0.01364. Times 400 gives approximately 111 or 122 prizes. Award for difference, both alternatives and counts.</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":8,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-APPLICATIONS-2022-ASSUMED-Q14":{"question":"<p>Six caravan sites A–F must be wired from a main power source; edge lengths are metres.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Draw a minimum spanning tree.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"drawing","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)(i)","text":"<p>If connection D-to-main must be included, find minimum wire length.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)(ii)","text":"<p>Find cost at$14.20/m.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Rock doubles the cost per metre on BF. How does the least-cost tree and total cost change?</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Uses a systematic spanning-tree algorithm and selects the five site edges of lengths 6.2,7.4,8.3,8.6,8.8, with the required connection to the main source. Award for method, some edges and complete tree.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(i)</td><td>Site tree 39.3 m plus main connection 6.4 m=45.7 m.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(ii)</td><td>45.7×14.20=$648.94.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>BF’s effective weight becomes 12.4, so replace BF by CF. Required wiring 48.2 m; cost$684.44. Award for removed edge, replacement and new cost.</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\"><img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":9,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2017-FREE-Q02":{"question":"<p>A soccer machine shoots uniformly across 15 m:3 m left of a 7 m goal and 5 m right. X is the crossing distance measured from the leftmost endpoint A; posts have negligible width.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Complete the density graph of X.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Find the probability of missing left.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Find the probability of entering the goal.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>Given a miss, find the probability it misses right.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Density 1/15 on[0,15], zero elsewhere; labels endpoints and height.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>3/15=1/5.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>7/15.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>(5/15)/(8/15)=5/8.</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\"><img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":6,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-APPLICATIONS-2021-ASSUMED-Q11":{"question":"<p>Pond algae increases by Cₙ₊₁=1.025 Cₙ, C₁=200 units/100 L. Judith treats it back to 200 when it exceeds 250.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)(i)","text":"<p>If treated Sunday 6 December 2020, find concentration Wednesday 9 December.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(a)(ii)","text":"<p>Find the next treatment day and date.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)(i)","text":"<p>January readings are 200,206,212.18,218.55,225.10,231.85,238.81. Find the revised recurrence.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)(ii)","text":"<p>Using that rule, find concentration on 20 January if last treated 10 January.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)(i)</td><td>200(1.025)³≈215.38 units/100 L.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(a)(ii)</td><td>First exceeds 250 after 10 daily increases; Wednesday 16 December 2020.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(i)</td><td>C₁=200, Cₙ₊₁=1.03 Cₙ.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(ii)</td><td>200(1.03)<sup>10</sup>≈268.78 units/100 L.</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":8,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2019-ASSUMED-Q17":{"question":"<p>Humpback killing was banned in 1963. The source gives population 30000 at the end of 2018 and labels this P(45), although the calendar gap is 55 years. Initially assume P′=0.1 P. For growth after 2018, a new model is P′=0.1 P−P²/700000, P(0)=30000.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Estimate the 1963 population under exponential growth.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Find a, b, c in P(t)=a/[1+be<sup>−ct</sup>]for the new model.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>During which year will the new population double?</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>State the major difference between the models.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>P=P₀e<sup>0.1 t</sup>. Using the given 45 years gives P₀=30000 e<sup>−4.5</sup>≈333. Using the correct 55-year calendar gap gives≈123; both are accepted by the official key. Award for model, substitution and estimate.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Carrying capacity a=70000; b=70000/30000−1=4/3; c=0.1.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>P=60000 gives e<sup>−0.1 t</sup>=1/8; t≈20.794 years after end 2018, so during 2039.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>The logistic model approaches 70000; exponential growth is unbounded.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":8,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2020-ASSUMED-Q15":{"question":"<p>Water in an oven follows T(t)=T₀−175 e<sup>−0.07 t</sup>°C, t minutes. The preliminary oven setting is 200°C; boiling requires 100°C in 5 minutes.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Find the initial water temperature.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Find its temperature after 5 minutes.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>What oven setting achieves boiling at 5 minutes?</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>For T₀=200, find T′(5)to 2 decimals.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(e)","text":"<p>Explain the long-term rate and temperature behaviour.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>25°C.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>200−175 e<sup>−0.35</sup>≈76.68°C.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>100=T₀−175 e<sup>−0.35</sup>, so T₀≈223.32°C.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>T′=12.25 e<sup>−0.07 t</sup>, giving≈8.63°C/min.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(e)</td><td>Rate remains positive but decreases toward 0; temperature keeps rising at a slowing rate and approaches the oven setting 200°C. Award for sign, rate limit and temperature limit.</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":9,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2020-ASSUMED-Q18":{"question":"<p>BikkiesAreUs biscuit chocolate mass is normal with mean 7.5 g and SD1.5 g.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Find P(total chocolate in 50 biscuits&lt;365 g), to 0.01.</p>","marks":4,"lines":12,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Find n such that P(|X̄−7.5|&lt;0.2)=0.98.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>A competitor samples 144 biscuits, SD1.8 g, total chocolate 1.09 kg. Assess its claim of significantly more chocolate per biscuit.</p>","marks":4,"lines":12,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Total mean 375 g, SD1.5√50; standardises(365−375)/(1.5√50), probability≈0.17. Award for total mean, total variance/SD, z and probability.</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Uses z₀.₉₉≈2.32635; n=(1.5 z/0.2)²≈304.42, so at least 305 biscuits. Award for quantile, equation and integer size.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Mean 1090/144≈7.5694 g; SE1.8/12=0.15. A95% interval≈(7.2754,7.8634)includes 7.5, so the sample does not support a significant increase. Award for mean, SE, interval/test and conclusion.</td><td>4</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":11,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2019-ASSUMED-Q08":{"question":"<p>A random survey finds 56 of 250 households do most grocery shopping at Big Foods.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)(i)","text":"<p>Find the sample proportion.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(a)(ii)","text":"<p>Find a 95% confidence interval, to 4 decimals.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(a)(iii)","text":"<p>Find the margin of error, to 4 decimals.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Independent research establishes the true proportion 0.17. Does(a)imply the junior surveyor made a mistake? Explain.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)(i)</td><td>56/250=0.224.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(a)(ii)</td><td>Uses 0.224±1.96√(0.224×0.776/250), giving≈(0.1723,0.2757). Award for SE, z and interval.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(a)(iii)</td><td>0.0517.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>No. A95% interval procedure misses the true proportion about 5% of the time; sampling variation can explain this interval’s small miss.</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":7,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-APPLICATIONS-2018-ASSUMED-Q14":{"question":"<p>Marco bought a$48000 vehicle, paid$5000 deposit and borrowed$43000 at 22.5% p. a. monthly, paying$1000 month-end. Three years have passed.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)(i)","text":"<p>Use a recurrence to find the current balance.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(a)(ii)","text":"<p>How much longer until repayment?</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>The car is now worth$27150. Find its average annual percentage depreciation.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>At purchase he could instead pay$1200 monthly at 22.5%, or$1000 monthly at 18.5%. Compare payoff time and total vehicle cost and recommend an option.</p>","marks":5,"lines":15,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)(i)</td><td>T₀=43000; Tₙ₊₁=1.01875 Tₙ−1000; T₃₆≈$33164.78. Award for recurrence, initial loan and balance.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(a)(ii)</td><td>Total 89 payments;53 additional months.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>(27150/48000)<sup>1/3</sup>≈0.827, so depreciation≈17.3% p. a.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>$1200 option:60 months, total repayments$71985.76, plus$5000 deposit=$76985.76. Reduced-rate option:72 months, total repayments$71078.56, plusdeposit=$76078.56. The latter saves$907.20; the former repays 12 months earlier. Accept a justified choice based on either priority. Award for both times, both totals and recommendation.</td><td>5</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":12,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-APPLICATIONS-2021-ASSUMED-Q15":{"question":"<p>Street upgrade costs between A–G are given in the cost matrix. A minimum spanning tree is required.</p><table><tr><th></th><th>A</th><th>B</th><th>C</th><th>D</th><th>E</th><th>F</th><th>G</th></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>—</td><td>7900</td><td>10300</td><td>—</td><td>7100</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>7900</td><td>—</td><td>7600</td><td>6200</td><td>6500</td><td>—</td><td>15400</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>10300</td><td>7600</td><td>—</td><td>8500</td><td>—</td><td>12200</td><td>9200</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td>—</td><td>6200</td><td>8500</td><td>—</td><td>8000</td><td>4700</td><td>9800</td></tr><tr><td>E</td><td>7100</td><td>6500</td><td>—</td><td>8000</td><td>—</td><td>4800</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>F</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>12200</td><td>4700</td><td>4800</td><td>—</td><td>10100</td></tr><tr><td>G</td><td>—</td><td>15400</td><td>9200</td><td>9800</td><td>—</td><td>10100</td><td>—</td></tr></table>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Show Prim’s algorithm and draw the tree.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"drawing","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Is the$42000 budget sufficient? Justify.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>If DCmust be included, how do the tree and minimum cost change?</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Selects AE7100, EF4800, FD4700, DB6200, BC7600, CG9200 using Prim’s algorithm. Award for method, some edges and complete tree.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Total$39600; budget is sufficient with$2400 remaining.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Replace BC7600 with DC8500; cost increases$900 to$40500.</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":7,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2018-ASSUMED-Q17":{"question":"<p>Tina believes 60% of her mangoes are large and samples 500 from a day’s picking.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Assuming her belief, state the approximate sample-proportion distribution.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Find P(p̂&lt;0.58).</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Describe how to select the 500 from a conveyor belt.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>A sample contains 250 large mangoes. Estimate the farm proportion.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(e)","text":"<p>Find its 95% confidence interval, to 4 decimals.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(f)","text":"<p>Does this support at least 60% large mangoes? Justify.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(g)","text":"<p>How can Tina further test the belief?</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>By normal approximation, p̂≈N(0.6,0.6×0.4/500). Award for distribution, mean and variance.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Standardises−0.02/√0.00048; probability≈0.1807.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Use a random start and systematic every-kth selection across the picking run, or independent random selections, avoiding size-based selection. Award for randomisation and representative coverage.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>0.5.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(e)</td><td>0.5±1.96√(0.25/500)≈(0.4562,0.5438). Award for formula, error and bounds.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(f)</td><td>No:0.60 lies outside and above the interval, so the data indicate a lower proportion.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(g)</td><td>Take further independent representative samples, preferably over multiple picking days.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":14,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-APPLICATIONS-2022-ASSUMED-Q11":{"question":"<p>Laundry drying-time data are shown in the graph and table. Temperatures are 28,35,42,31,24,21,21,35,32,28°C; times are 29,20,15,27,34,38,35,18, blank, blank minutes.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Read the two missing times from the graph.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Find the least-squares regression of time on temperature and r.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Draw the regression line.</p>","marks":2,"lines":8,"space":"drawing","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>Describe direction and strength.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(e)","text":"<p>What percentage of drying-time variation is explained by temperature?</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(f)","text":"<p>Give another plausible factor.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(g)(i)","text":"<p>Predict drying time at 17°C.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(g)(ii)","text":"<p>Is the prediction reliable? Explain.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>24 and 32 minutes, respectively.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Time≈−1.13 Temperature+60.74; r≈−0.97.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Line passes near(28,29)with slope≈−1.13.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Strong negative association.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(e)</td><td>Approximately 94.5%.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(f)</td><td>For example wind, humidity, fabric type or shade.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(g)(i)</td><td>≈41.53 minutes.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(g)(ii)</td><td>Less reliable because 17°Cis below the observed range; this is extrapolation.</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":13,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2021-ASSUMED-Q14":{"question":"<p>Cinema tickets cost whole-dollar amounts a for adults, c for children, p for pensioners. Groups pay:2 adults+4 children=$108;3 adults+6 children=$162;2 adults+5 children+2 pensioners=$152.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Write the three equations.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Show c+2 p=44.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Find a general solution.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>Describe the geometry of the three planes.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(e)","text":"<p>Given a&gt; c&gt; p&gt;0, find the ticket prices.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>2 a+4 c=108;3 a+6 c=162;2 a+5 c+2 p=152.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Subtracts the first equation from the third, obtaining c+2 p=44.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>For free parameter c, a=54−2 c, p=22−c/2.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>First two planes coincide; the third intersects them in a line, so there are infinitely many real solutions.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(e)</td><td>Inequalities give 44/3&lt; c&lt;18. Integer p requires even c, so c=16, a=22, p=14. Award for bounds, integer restriction and prices.</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":10,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2023-FREE-Q03":{"question":"<p>Twenty solar farms have generating capacities W(MW) with histogram frequencies 4,8,6,2 in intervals 5–15,15–25,25–35,35–45. A second variable is Y=aW, a&gt;0.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Complete cumulative probabilities at 5,15,25,35,45.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Find P(W≥35).</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)(i)","text":"<p>Estimate P(W≥20).</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)(ii)","text":"<p>Estimate E(W).</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>Given Var(W)=81, Var(Y)=324, find E(Y).</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>0,0.2,0.6,0.9,1; award for at least three then all.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>0.1.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)(i)</td><td>Interpolates halfway through 15–25; probability=0.2+0.3+0.1=0.6.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(c)(ii)</td><td>Uses midpoints:10(0.2)+20(0.4)+30(0.3)+40(0.1)=23 MW.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>324=a²81; a=2; E(Y)=2×23=46 MW. One mark each.</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":10,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-APPLICATIONS-2020-ASSUMED-Q12":{"question":"<p>Jessica borrows$15000 from her parents at 4% p. a., initially compounded yearly.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)(i)","text":"<p>Complete balances after years 1,2,3 without repayments.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(a)(ii)","text":"<p>Write the annual recurrence.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>If she repays$2400 at each year-end, write the recurrence.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)(i)","text":"<p>If interest is monthly and payments are$200 monthly, write the recurrence.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)(ii)","text":"<p>How many months until repayment?</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)(iii)","text":"<p>Find total repayments, including the final partial payment.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)(i)</td><td>$15600,$16224,$16872.96; award for first then all.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(a)(ii)</td><td>A₀=15000, Aₙ₊₁=1.04 Aₙ.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>T₀=15000, Tₙ₊₁=1.04 Tₙ−2400.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)(i)</td><td>B₀=15000, Bₙ₊₁=(1+0.04/12)Bₙ−200.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)(ii)</td><td>87 months.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)(iii)</td><td>Balance after 86 payments≈$89.46; final interest≈$0.30; total 86×200+89.76=$17289.76. One mark each.</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":10,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2016-ASSUMED-Q10":{"question":"<p>A WA survey finds 986 of 1450 Year 12 students use a graphics calculator. Other surveys report 1772/3221,1021/1566, and 2203/3221.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Find a 90% confidence interval for the WA proportion, stating assumptions.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Find its margin of error.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Which other surveys are more likely from outside WA? Justify.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>Find the sample size needed to halve the margin at the same confidence level.</p>","marks":4,"lines":12,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>p̂=0.68; assumes random representative independent sampling;0.68±1.645√(0.68×0.32/1450)≈(0.660,0.700). Award for estimate, assumptions and bounds.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>E≈0.02015.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Other proportions≈0.5501,0.6520,0.6840. First two lie outside the WA interval, so are more likely outside WA; the third is consistent. Award for proportions, comparison and conclusion.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>E=z√[p(1−p)/n], so E∝n<sup>−1/2</sup>. Halving E requires four times the size:5800. Award for formula, scaling, equation and result.</td><td>4</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":12,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2017-ASSUMED-Q18":{"question":"<p>A child rides a radius 5 m roundabout with period 12 seconds. A parent stands 3 m beyond the nearest rim point B, so OP=8 m. Angle θ is measured from OB; s is parent–child distance.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Find dθ/dt.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Show s²=89−80 cosθ.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Find ds/dt after 4 seconds.</p>","marks":4,"lines":12,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>Show cosθ=5/8 when ds/dt is greatest.</p>","marks":4,"lines":12,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>2π/12=π/6 rad/s.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Cosine rule: s²=5²+8²−2×5×8 cosθ.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Differentiates:2 ss′=80 sinθ·θ′. At 4 seconds θ=2π/3, s=√129, so s′=10π√3/(3√129)≈1.597 m/s. Award for differentiation, angle, distance and rate.</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Maximises (1−u²)/(89−80 u), u=cosθ. Derivative numerator 80 u²−178 u+80=0 gives u=5/8 or 8/5. Rejects 8/5 outside[−1,1];5/8 gives maximum. Award for objective, derivative, roots and validation.</td><td>4</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":10,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2018-ASSUMED-Q12":{"question":"<p>Parcel weight X~N(3,1²)kg. Postage Y is$5 for X≤1,$6.50 for 1&lt; X≤2,$8 for 2&lt; X≤3,$9.50 for 3&lt; X≤4, and$12 for X&gt;4.</p><table><tr><th>X(kg)</th><th>≤1</th><th>1–2</th><th>2–3</th><th>3–4</th><th>>4</th></tr><tr><td>Y($)</td><td>5</td><td>6.5</td><td>8</td><td>9.5</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>Probability</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></table>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>What percentage weigh more than 3.7 kg?</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>For 20 independent parcels, find the probability at least 10 exceed 3.7 kg.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Construct the probability distribution of Y.</p>","marks":4,"lines":12,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>Find E(Y).</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(e)","text":"<p>Find SD(Y).</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(f)","text":"<p>New postage is 1.2 Y+1. Find its mean and SD.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(g)","text":"<p>Explain a limitation of the normal weight model.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>P(Z&gt;0.7)≈0.241964, or 24.20%.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>K~Bin(20,0.241964); evaluates∑<sub>k=10</sub><sup>20</sup>C(20, k)pᵏ(1−p)<sup>20−k</sup>. Award for binomial model, event and calculator evaluation≈0.010953.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>For Y=5,6.5,8,9.5,12, the probabilities are approximately 0.022750,0.135905,0.341345,0.341345,0.158655. Award for normal boundaries and three correct distribution groups.</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>E(Y)=∑yp(y), using the distribution above; correct substitution and evaluation≈$8.8745.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(e)</td><td>SD(Y)=√[∑y²p(y)−E(Y)²]; award for second moment, variance and square root≈$1.7471.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(f)</td><td>Mean=1.2 E(Y)+1; SD=1.2 SD(Y). Award for mean transformation, scale-only SD and numerical evaluation(mean≈$11.6494, SD≈$2.0965).</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(g)</td><td>Normal support includes negative weights, which are impossible; P(X&lt;0)≈0.00135.</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":19,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2024-ASSUMED-Q16":{"question":"<p>Karlie’s glide is r=(-200,300,700)+t(2,-1,-1), and Ben’s is r=(500,-200,800)+u(-0.5,1,-1.5), in metres and seconds.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Find Ben’s speed.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Find Ben’s angle of descent to the horizontal.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Find the shortest distance between their flight paths.</p>","marks":4,"lines":12,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>|v|=√(0.25+1+2.25)=√3.5≈1.87 m/s.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>sinα=1.5/√3.5, so α≈53.3°. Award for vertical component, speed ratio and angle.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Directions cross to n=(2.5,3.5,1.5); joining vector(700,-500,100). Distance=|joining·n|/|n|=150/√20.75≈32.93 m. Award for normal, joining vector, projection and result.</td><td>4</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":9,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2022-ASSUMED-Q13":{"question":"<p>Melbourne has 35% of people brown-eyed. A survey of 100 people is taken. A Perth survey of 243 gives a confidence interval(0.2520,0.3488).</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Using normality, find P(p̂&lt;0.28)for Melbourne.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Explain two biases if sampling outside one shop on Tuesday 9–10 am.</p>","marks":4,"lines":12,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>How many Perth respondents had brown eyes?</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>Find the confidence level of the Perth interval.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>p̂≈N(0.35,0.35×0.65/100); z≈−1.468; probability≈0.0711. One mark each.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>One shop/location may attract a particular population; weekday morning excludes people working or elsewhere then. Two marks per explained bias.</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Centre 0.3004;243×0.3004≈73 people.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Half-width 0.0484; SE≈√(0.3004×0.6996/243)≈0.0294; z≈1.645, so 90% confidence.</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":12,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2017-ASSUMED-Q13":{"question":"<p>A random sample of 100 cables has mean breaking strength 10300 N and sample SD400 N. Required strength is 10000 N.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Find a 95% confidence interval for population mean.</p>","marks":4,"lines":12,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)(i)","text":"<p>Does this guarantee another sample mean lies in the interval? Explain.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)(ii)","text":"<p>Does it guarantee individual cables exceed 10000 N? Explain.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Why is normal inference reasonable without a normal population?</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>A sample of 36 with SD500 N gives interval(9900,10200). Find its confidence level.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>SE=400/√100=40; E=1.96×40=78.4; interval(10221.6,10378.4)N. Award for SE, z, error and bounds.</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(i)</td><td>No; another sample mean varies randomly and need not lie in this realised interval.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(ii)</td><td>No; the interval estimates the population mean, not individual strengths, which vary more widely.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>CLT: with 100 independent observations, the sample mean is approximately normal.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>E=150, SE=500/6; z=1.8. Confidence 2Φ(1.8)−1≈92.8%. One mark each.</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":13,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2018-ASSUMED-Q16":{"question":"<p>A random sample of 400 measurements gives a 99% confidence interval with upper endpoint 40.62 and width 1.08. Three students’ intervals are graphed: Tom’s is widest.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Find the sample mean.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Find the sample SD.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)(i)","text":"<p>At the same sample size and confidence, does Tom have the largest SD? Explain.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)(ii)","text":"<p>Do different sample means imply different populations? Explain.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>Can the graph identify which intervals contain the true mean? Explain.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>Lower endpoint 39.54; midpoint 40.08.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>E=0.54, z≈2.57583; SE=0.54/z; SD=SE√400≈4.19. One mark each.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(c)(i)</td><td>Yes: equal n and confidence make width proportional to sample SD.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)(ii)</td><td>No; sampling variation can give different means from the same population.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>No. The true mean is unknown;99% describes repeated-sampling coverage, not which realised intervals succeed.</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":9,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2019-ASSUMED-Q15":{"question":"<p>August commuters: sample size n, mean 40 minutes, sample-mean SD3 minutes. November: sample size 2 n, mean 45 minutes, from the same population.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Find the August 90% confidence interval, to 0.01 minute.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Find the November sample-mean SD, to 0.01 minute.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Which 90% interval is most precise: August, November, or their combined 3 n sample? Explain.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>Which interval contains the true mean? Explain.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>40±1.64485×3≈(35.07,44.93)minutes. Award for z, error and bounds.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>3/√2≈2.12 minutes.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Combined sample: largest n gives the smallest SE and interval width.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Cannot determine:μ is unknown;90% is long-run coverage of the procedure.</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":9,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2019-ASSUMED-Q10":{"question":"<p>In a 200-person sibling survey, X=0,1,2,3 has probabilities 0.2, a, b,0.1. The initially reported mean is 1.3. Later the distribution is corrected to 0.2,0.3,0.4,0.1.</p><table><tr><th>x</th><th>0</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th></tr><tr><td>Corrected P(X=x)</td><td>0.2</td><td>0.3</td><td>0.4</td><td>0.1</td></tr><tr><td>Participants</td><td>40</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></table>","parts":[{"label":"(a)(i)","text":"<p>Write two independent equations in a, b using the original report.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(a)(ii)","text":"<p>Solve them.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)(i)","text":"<p>Find participant counts from the corrected distribution.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)(ii)","text":"<p>Find the corrected mean and SD.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)(i)</td><td>a+b=0.7; a+2 b+0.3=1.3.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(a)(ii)</td><td>a=0.4, b=0.3.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(i)</td><td>40,60,80,20 participants.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(ii)</td><td>E(X)=1.4; E(X²)=2.8; SD=√(2.8−1.96)=√0.84≈0.9165.</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":7,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2020-ASSUMED-Q17":{"question":"<p>A sheep enclosure has perpendicular sides EF=p, FG=q, p+q=500 m. With F the origin and FG the x-axis, its road boundary is R(x)=10 sin(x/15)+p,0≤x≤q.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Show its area is A(q)=500 q−150 cos(q/15)−q²+150.</p>","marks":4,"lines":12,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Find the q maximising area, to the nearest metre, and the maximum area.</p>","marks":4,"lines":12,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)(i)","text":"<p>Find R′(x).</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)(ii)","text":"<p>Using arc length∫<sub>0</sub><sup>q</sup>√[1+R′(x)²]dx, find total fencing for this enclosure.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>A=∫[10 sin(x/15)+p]dx from 0 to q=pq−150 cos(q/15)+150. Substitute p=500−q to obtain the result. Award for integral, evaluation, constraint and substitution.</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>A′=500+10 sin(q/15)−2 q=0 gives q≈247 m. A″=(2/3)cos(q/15)−2&lt;0 throughout, so this is the maximum; area≈62750 m². One mark per step.</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>(c)(i)</td><td>R′(x)=(2/3)cos(x/15).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)(ii)</td><td>Using rounded q 247: p 253, road arc≈273 m, right sideR(247)≈247 m. Total≈253+247+273+247=1020 m. Award for arc, right side and total.</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":12,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2017-ASSUMED-Q19":{"question":"<p>A file-processing time T~N(90,15²)minutes. Outsourcing cost Y is$200 ifT≤60,$600 if 60&lt; T&lt;120, and$1200 ifT≥120.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Find P(T&gt;120).</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Find the probability exactly two of five independent days exceed 120 minutes.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)(i)","text":"<p>Complete the distribution of Y.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)(ii)","text":"<p>Find the mean cost.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)(iii)","text":"<p>Find the SD of cost.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)(iv)","text":"<p>If next year’s cost is N=aY+b, state its mean and SD.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>P(Z&gt;2)≈0.0227501.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>Binomial probability C(5,2)(0.0227501)²(0.9772499)³≈0.00484. Award for model, expression and result.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(c)(i)</td><td>Probabilities 0.0227501,0.9544997,0.0227501, respectively.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)(ii)</td><td>E(Y)=200 p+600(1−2 p)+1200 p≈$604.55.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(c)(iii)</td><td>√[200²p+600²(1−2 p)+1200²p−604.55²]≈$108.67.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(c)(iv)</td><td>Mean=aE(Y)+b; SD=|a|SD(Y), or aSD(Y)for a&gt;0.</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":12,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2021-ASSUMED-Q16":{"question":"<p>Profit in millions after x weeks is P(x)=20 ln(x+a)/(x+5), initially 4. After 10 weeks, a new model is N(y)=2 e<sup>b(10+y)</sup>, y weeks after the change, with continuity at the change.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Show a=e.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Predict profit after 5 weeks.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Use quotient rule to obtain an equation for maximum profit.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>State maximum profit and the week when it occurs.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(e)","text":"<p>When does profit fall below its initial value?</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(f)","text":"<p>Under the new model, when does profit exceed$5 million?</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>4=20 ln(a)/5, so ln(a)=1, a=e.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>P(5)≈4.087 million dollars.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>P′=[20(x+5)/(x+e)−20 ln(x+e)]/(x+5)². Sets numerator equal to 0. Award for quotient rule, derivative and equation.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>x≈1.79, during week 2; maximum≈$4.436 million.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(e)</td><td>Positive solution P(x)=4 is x≈5.581, so during week 6.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(f)</td><td>P(10)≈3.39072=N(0); b=ln[P(10)/2]/10≈0.05279. Solve N(y)=5 to get y≈7.36, during week 8 after the change. One mark each.</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":12,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2021-ASSUMED-Q17":{"question":"<p>Online spending sample: size n, mean$400, SDs; its 95% confidence interval has width$200. Intervals A: n, s,95%; B: n, s,99%; C:2 n, s,95%; D: n,0.8 s,95%.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>State the initial interval.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Find the SD of the sample mean, to$0.01.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>What sample size reduces the 95% interval width to$50?</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>For size 2 n, find P(|X̄−μ|&gt;50).</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(e)","text":"<p>Which of A–Dcontains μ? Justify.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(f)(i)","text":"<p>Which is narrower, AorB? Explain.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(f)(ii)","text":"<p>Which is narrower, CorD? Explain.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>($300,$500).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>SE=100/1.96≈$51.02.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Width divided by 4 requires n multiplied by 16; size 16 n.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>SE₂=100/(1.96√2); z=50/SE₂≈1.386; two-sided probability≈0.166. Award for SE, z and both tails.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(e)</td><td>Cannot know which realised intervals contain the unknown fixed μ; confidence specifies method coverage.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(f)(i)</td><td>A:95% uses a smaller critical value than 99%.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(f)(ii)</td><td>C: width factor 1/√2≈0.707 is less than D’s 0.8.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":12,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-SPECIALIST-2022-ASSUMED-Q17":{"question":"<p>A vase is formed by revolving x=15−4 cos(πy/40),0≤y≤40, about the y-axis. It contains water to 6 cm and must reach 32 cm. Added volume V(cm³)obeys V′=300 e<sup>−V/12000</sup>, V(0)=0.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Find the volume to add, nearest cm³.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>After 6000 cm³has been added, estimate the next 0.5 seconds’ volume by increments.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Find flow rate when depth reaches 32 cm.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)","text":"<p>Separate variables to obtain V(t).</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>π∫<sub>6</sub><sup>32</sup>[15−4 cos(πy/40)]²dy≈18107.94, so 18108 cm³. Award for limits, disc integrand and volume.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>ΔV≈300 e<sup>−6000/12000</sup>×0.5≈91 cm³.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>300 e<sup>−18107.94/12000</sup>≈66 cm³/s.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>∫e<sup>V/12000</sup>dV=∫300 dt;12000 e<sup>V/12000</sup>=300 t+C, C=12000. Hence V=12000 ln(1+t/40). Award for separation, integration and initial condition/rule.</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":10,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-APPLICATIONS-2018-ASSUMED-Q11":{"question":"<p>Charles is 60 with$465000. He wants$675000 at 65, investing at 3.35% p. a. monthly with month-end deposits. At 65 he plans yearly$65000 withdrawals from a fund earning 3.25% p. a. monthly.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Find the required monthly deposit.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)(i)","text":"<p>How many full yearly annuity payments can the fund provide?</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)(ii)","text":"<p>Find the nominal annual rate, compounded monthly, needed for 20 yearly payments to age 85.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>j=.0335/12; uses 60 months and 675000=465000(1+j)<sup>60</sup>+PMT[(1+j)<sup>60</sup>−1]/j; PMT≈$1921.80. One mark each.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(i)</td><td>Annual effective rate(1+.0325/12)<sup>12</sup>−1; annuity equation gives N≈12.92;12 full payments. Award for rate, payment periods and rounding down.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(b)(ii)</td><td>Solves 675000=65000[1−(1+r/12)<sup>−240</sup>]/[(1+r/12)<sup>12</sup>−1]; r≈7.03% p. a.</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":8,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-METHODS-2021-ASSUMED-Q17":{"question":"<p>A cable car initially at rest travels up to the mountain station, stops, then returns to the resort and stops. Acceleration a(t)=kt²−23 t+20 k m/min². At 2 minutes v=18 m/min and it is still ascending.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>Find k.</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>How long from mountain to resort?</p>","marks":3,"lines":9,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>It breaks down 10 minutes into the test. How far from the mountain station is it?</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>v=kt³/3−23 t²/2+20 kt, using v(0)=0. Substitute v(2)=18 to get k=1.5. Award for integration, substitution and result.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>v=0.5 t³−11.5 t²+30 t=0 at t=0,3,20. Mountain reached at 3, resort at 20; return 17 minutes. Award for equation, roots and difference.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Distance=|∫<sub>3</sub><sup>10</sup>(0.5 t³−11.5 t²+30 t)dt|≈1125 m below mountain station.</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"<img src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Original examination graph or diagram\" data-word-max-height=\"3.2\">","marks":8,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true},"WV-MATHS-APPLICATIONS-2018-ASSUMED-Q09":{"question":"<p>Mealworms follow Mₙ₊₁=0.9(Mₙ−12)+15, M₁=50. After 10 weeks hot weather changes the model to Nₙ₊₁=0.8(Nₙ−12)+15, N₁=c. Later the vet suggests 10 worms/week and a steady stock 30, using Pₙ₊₁=0.8(Pₙ−10)+k.</p>","parts":[{"label":"(a)","text":"<p>What percentage dies weekly under the first model?</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(b)","text":"<p>Estimate stock at the start of week 5.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(c)","text":"<p>Justify that this model never runs out.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)(i)","text":"<p>Find c.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(d)(ii)","text":"<p>Estimate stock at the start of week 30.</p>","marks":1,"lines":4,"space":"lines","diagram":""},{"label":"(e)","text":"<p>Find k for the desired steady stock.</p>","marks":2,"lines":6,"space":"lines","diagram":""}],"answer":"<table class=\"editable-source-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Solution and marking criterion</th><th>Marks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>10%.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>M₅≈47.249; about 48 available if rounding up as in the source key(47 nearest integer also reflects the continuous model).</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>Fixed point L=0.9(L−12)+15 gives 42. Starting 50, the sequence decreases toward 42, remaining positive.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>(d)(i)</td><td>c=M₁₁=42+8(0.9)<sup>10</sup>≈44.789.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(d)(ii)</td><td>Approximately 27 mealworms.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>(e)</td><td>30=0.8(30−10)+k, so k=14.</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table>","diagram":"","marks":8,"responseLines":0,"reviewedAgainstSource":true}});
